---
title: My Awesome Homepage
theme: dark
color: slate
headerStyle: clean
showStats: false
target: _blank
# Group order here IS display order, and a group services.yaml declares but this block omits
# renders below the laid-out ones at one column wide. `Tracking` sat in exactly that state
# until 2026-09-09, and a `Monitoring:` entry sat here naming a group services.yaml has never
# declared — dead either way, because layout entries match a group by name.
#
# The split is by WIDGET, not by topic. A widgeted tile is several lines tall and a link-only
# tile is one line, so mixing them in one group leaves ragged holes. Services, Calendar and
# Media hold the widgeted tiles; Admin and Tools hold the link-only ones, four to a row.
layout:
  Top Row:
    style: row
    columns: 2
    header: false
    # THREE columns, and the number is derived from the tile count rather than chosen. Services
    # holds nine tiles. At two columns that is four full rows plus an orphan — Home Assistant
    # alone on row five with a 424x120 hole beside it — and a 600px-tall column against a
    # 372px Calendar, leaving 228px dead under the Calendar. Nine divides by three, so three
    # columns gives three full rows, no orphan, and a 396px column that all but matches the
    # Calendar. Measured in the browser 2026-09-09 at 1920px and 1280px: tiles land at 280px
    # and 195px wide with no tile overflowing its box.
    #
    # Keep this in step with the tile count. Adding a tenth widgeted tile here reintroduces the
    # orphan; either add two, or move one out to Media.
    Services:
      style: row
      columns: 3
    Calendar:
      style: column
      columns: 1
    # DO NOT PUT `Bookmarks:` HERE. A BOOKMARK GROUP CANNOT LIVE INSIDE `Top Row` AT ALL, and
    # homepage says nothing when it refuses. Both depths were tried and measured on the deployed
    # page, 2026-09-09:
    #
    #   - nested under `Calendar:`  -> ignored
    #   - a sibling of Services and Calendar -> ignored
    #
    # Both times the group fell out of Top Row and rendered LAST at full width (1728px), which
    # is exactly what an unlaid-out group does. Nothing logs it, `k8s_manifests.py` stays at
    # 287/287, and the homepage guards stay at 12 passed — only reading the rendered page shows
    # it. A bookmark group is only ever laid out as a TOP-LEVEL entry, which is where it now is.
    #
    # So the calendar column cannot hold the bookmarks. To put something under the calendar, it
    # has to be a SERVICE tile in services.yaml.j2's `Calendar` group, not a bookmark.
  Media:
    style: row
    columns: 4
  Admin:
    style: row
    columns: 4
  Tools:
    style: row
    columns: 4
  # Top level is the ONLY place a bookmark group is laid out — see the note in `Top Row` above
  # for the two arrangements that were tried there and silently ignored.
  Bookmarks:
    style: row
    columns: 5
  Tracking:
    style: row
    columns: 2
# Type-to-filter over every tile and bookmark on the page. This is what keeps the link-only
# Admin and Tools groups usable as they grow — the keyboard reaches a service whether or not
# its tile is in view. searchDescriptions widens the match to the `description:` line, so
# "download" finds qBittorrent through its "Download management" text.
quicklaunch:
  searchDescriptions: true
  hideInternetSearch: false
  hideVisitURL: false
providers:
  openweathermap: {{ weather_api_key }}
  # The longhorn INFO WIDGET reads its URL from here, not from its own entry in widgets.yaml.
  # Homepage splits that widget across the two files — `providers.longhorn` holds the connection,
  # widgets.yaml holds only the display options — and a `url:` written beside those options is
  # ignored, leaving the pod logging `<longhorn> Missing Longhorn URL` every refresh behind a
  # 1/1 Deployment. Measured 2026-09-06: that is exactly how it shipped in PR #1391.
  #
  # longhorn-frontend, not longhorn-backend, and the reasoning is in widgets.yaml.j2 beside the
  # display options.
  longhorn:
    url: http://longhorn-frontend.longhorn-system.svc.cluster.local
